%%WIKI-SYNC-META%%
title: c1.1 Pepper, resident profiles and local server
syntax: xwiki/2.1
%%WIKI-SYNC-META%%
**Abstract.** Everything runs inside the care home, with no internet connection. Five options are selected: Pepper in the lounge (TECH-01), presence sensing in residents' rooms, opt-in only (TECH-03), a local server with one instance of the System per resident (TECH-04), consent-gated matching (TECH-05) and a resident profile for matching (TECH-06). Four are rejected: the pocket guide (TECH-02), cloud speech and language services (TECH-07), audio recording (TECH-08), and location and body sensing (TECH-09). No hands-on testing yet.

= Technology Option - Pepper in the lounge (selected) [TECH-01] =

**Report Format:** §1.3 Technology

== Fields ==

|=Field|=Description
|ID|TECH-01
|Name|Pepper in the lounge (selected)
|Status|In Progress
|Evidence basis|Vendor documentation; the course platform. No hands-on testing by this project.
|Category|Social Robot
|Capabilities|(((
Humanoid; speech and a chest tablet.

* Asks each resident alone, by voice, with yes and no on the tablet (F1)
* Introduces two residents by name and photo, mentions one shared item, and suggests something to do together (F3)
* Welcomes newcomers, and repeats names at later meetings (F3)
* Defers sensitive questions to the trusted person (F4)
)))
|Limitations|Flat indoor floors only. Speech recognition of older voices is unreliable, so residents can answer on the tablet (O-3). The humanoid form may read as a supervisor. Cameras present (covered). A lounge is not private: asking needs a quiet corner (O-8).
|Maturity and feasibility|Commercially available; supplied by the course.
|Failure and fallback behaviour|Pepper unavailable: the coordinator or a care worker asks and introduces, from the same match panel.
|Benefits|HV-01: each resident is asked face to face, in private. HV-06: introductions that staff have no time for.
|Drawbacks|Strains HV-03 (cameras); mitigated by disabling and visibly covering them. Some residents may find introductions by a robot odd, or childish.
|Selected or rejected|Selected, in the lounge, near its entrance.

== Graph edges ==

enables → F1, F3, F4; addresses → HV-01, HV-06; realized-in → RP-01 (§2.2).

= Technology Option - Pocket guide (rejected) [TECH-02] =

**Report Format:** §1.3 Technology

== Fields ==

|=Field|=Description
|ID|TECH-02
|Name|Pocket guide (rejected)
|Status|In Progress
|Evidence basis|Group reasoning. Off-the-shelf parts.
|Category|Wearable / AI-ICT
|Capabilities|A palm-sized device on a rollator clip or a lanyard, with a speaker, a microphone, two buttons and motion sensing.
|Limitations|Built for keeping conversations going on walks; it has to listen to do so.
|Maturity and feasibility|Prototype on a phone; feasible.
|Failure and fallback behaviour|Not applicable - rejected.
|Benefits|HV-06: could remind a resident of an agreed meeting.
|Drawbacks|A listening device carried everywhere strains HV-03. Reminders work as well through staff (F5) and Pepper, with no device on the resident.
|Selected or rejected|Rejected.

= Technology Option - Presence sensing in residents' rooms (selected, opt-in only) [TECH-03] =

**Report Format:** §1.3 Technology

== Fields ==

|=Field|=Description
|ID|TECH-03
|Name|Presence sensing in residents' rooms (selected, opt-in only)
|Status|In Progress
|Evidence basis|Group reasoning. Door sensors and Bluetooth beacons are standard parts. Concerns about surveillance in residential care reviewed in Niemeijer et al. (2010).
|Category|Sensing
|Capabilities|(((
A small sensor at the door of an opted-in resident's room, or a beacon the resident chooses to carry, registers whether they are in the room.

The server keeps one number per day: hours spent in the room. When the count stays above the resident's usual level for several days [threshold - O-2], the named care worker receives a note (F5).
)))
|Limitations|Cannot tell sleep from reading, or a visitor from solitude. A few days in bed with a cold look like withdrawal. Only as good as its threshold.
|Maturity and feasibility|High; standard parts.
|Failure and fallback behaviour|Sensor silent: no count and no note; staff observation continues as today.
|Benefits|HV-05, HV-06: withdrawal noticed before mood and mobility decline (PS-02).
|Drawbacks|(((
Intrudes on private time, and can cross a boundary: a visit prompted by a count can feel like being watched (VT-06). A record of where a resident is, however coarse, is a privacy risk (HV-03).

Mitigations: opt-in only, with the resident's assent (O-7); one number a day, no timeline; notes to one named care worker, who calls in to offer company, never to check; the System never contacts the resident about it; the resident can stop it at any time; the setting is reviewed at every care-plan review.
)))
|Selected or rejected|Selected, opt-in only.

== Graph edges ==

enables → F5; addresses → HV-05, HV-06; strains → VT-06.

= Technology Option - Local server, one instance per resident (selected) [TECH-04] =

**Report Format:** §1.3 Technology

== Fields ==

|=Field|=Description
|ID|TECH-04
|Name|Local server, one instance per resident (selected)
|Status|In Progress
|Evidence basis|Group reasoning. Care homes already run local networks and work phones.
|Category|AI/ICT
|Capabilities|(((
* One instance per resident, in its own encrypted store: profile (TECH-06), settings, consent register
* The relationship map (TECH-05)
* Notes to staff work phones over the home's Wi-Fi
* Session record (F6)
* No internet connection; updates by signed package, installed on site
)))
|Limitations|A single point of failure. No remote maintenance. Backups on site.
|Maturity and feasibility|High; standard parts. Run by ST-07.
|Failure and fallback behaviour|Server down: no suggestions and no notes, so staff introduce residents as today. Staff phones show the state.
|Benefits|HV-03: nothing leaves the home. HV-01: each resident's own settings.
|Drawbacks|Maintenance load on ST-07.
|Selected or rejected|Selected.

